 > The two most fundamental concepts are "actors" and
 > "events."

This is a pretty popular idea in a certain
community. I seem to remember there even being a
programming language called "actor" or something
very like that.

 > An "event" is something that happens which changes
 > the state of the game - something takes damage,
 > something is opened, something is broken, etc.
 > Events involve up to six actors; a subject (who's
 > doing the action), an object (who or which it's
 > being done to), an agent (weapon or tool that's
 > being used to do it), an agency (conveyor of
 > effect, secondary tool, or ammo), an instigator
 > (the creature or item "responsible" for the
 > action, for purposes of experience awarded,
 > conducts, alignment, or anger/fear responses I
 > haven't coded yet) and a timing reference (an
 > actor which, if sped, slowed, paralyzed, or
 > destroyed, will accordingly change the timing of
 > the event or cancel it).  Usually the instigator,
 > subject, and timing reference are the same actor,
 > but they don't have to be.

 > Each actor has a resizable hash table to hold its
 > special properties, which includes all kinds of
 > things, but most interestingly "triggers."  A
 > trigger is hashed by a combination of event and
 > role within that event, designated "before" or
 > "after", and contains several parameters plus a
 > pointer to an executable method.  During normal
 > event processing, the event and all actors' roles
 > in the event are known, and the system checks for
 > triggers on all the actors involved, jumping to
 > and executing their triggered methods if they have
 > any.  The methods can modify the impending event
 > if they're "before" methods.  They can also
 > schedule new events, queue messages to the user,
 > or do some other things. But they're not allowed
 > to directly modify any actors; only events should
 > do that.

 > As an example, if we have a FrostBrand-type sword,
 > It'll have a trigger keyed something like
 > TR_BEFORE_MWATTACK_AGENT (meaning, something to do
 > before being used as an agent by an EV_MWATTACK
 > event) storing some parameters and a code pointer.
 > When we get the next event from the schedule, and
 > it's an EV_MWATTACK (meelee attack with a weapon)
 > we check the actors involved for
 > TR_BEFORE_<event>_<role> triggers corresponding to
 > their role and that event.  We find that the agent
 > (weapon) has a TR_BEFORE_MWATTACK_AGENT trigger,
 > so we jump to its method using the pointer stored
 > under that trigger.  The routine gets pointers to
 > the EV_MWATTACK event record and the
 > TR_BEFORE_MWATTACK_AGENT hash table record, so it
 > has access to everything in both records as
 > parameters, notably including the identities of
 > other actors in the event. It can modify the event
 > adding extra cold damage before the event actually
 > happens.  Then we jump to the code pointer in the
 > EV_MWATTACK event, which executes the (modified)
 > event itself (it gets a pointer to the event
 > record as its parameter so all the bundled
 > arguments are passed to it).  Then we check for
 > and execute "after" triggers, and we're done with
 > one event.  We get the next event from the
 > schedule, rinse, repeat until game over.

 > The idea is that this general property check,
 > which is done in one place by the event processing
 > code, provides the attachment point for any and
 > all special behavior that is reasonable to want to
 > attach to any particular actor.  And yes, an actor
 > can have multiple triggers on the same role and
 > the same event. If so they all get called.

 > To add a special case, you select a trigger
 > method, or write one from scratch, then just
 > insert a property on the specialized actor
 > pointing to that code and keyed by the event and
 > the actor's role in that event. You don't wind up
 > interfering with other special-behavior patches
 > because you don't even touch the main execution
 > sequence.  The program finds the trigger when it's
 > doing normal event processing and jumps to your
 > method, either before or after the event is
 > processed as you specify.  You can also add event
 > types, and if you do the makefile will pick them
 > up and create corresponding triggers for them, so
 > you don't have to worry about keeping all of this
 > machinery synchronized by hand.

 > Some events are "virtual."  The actual event code
 > in this case does nothing, but processing the
 > event causes a check for and execution of
 > triggered specializations that do all the work.

 > Here I've been talking about "Code pointers" but
 > the actual implementation uses integer indices
 > into vectors of code pointers, both for triggered
 > methods and event code.  The declarations of these
 > vectors of code pointers are once again
 > autogenerated by the makefile; you define the
 > routine, the build process will put pointers to it
 > into the tables.  This allows both the schedule
 > (containing all events that haven't happened yet)
 > and the actors (with their property tables) to be
 > reliably serialized.

I like what I read, although the term "pointer" hits
a bad allergy of mine, but there are easier ways to
do what you wanted than by embedding it into the
build process.

That sounds something like the "register" process of
OOPLs, where a bit of code that wants to be in the
chain of execution of some supervisory code gets
itself added at code initialization time to a queue
which that supervisory code walks each time an event
arrives. to see which attachee wants to "eat" that
event, though the attachee can choose to process the
even but not consume it, so that other attachees
further down the chain also get a shot at the event.

